  "query_preview": "(Today through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 253 AM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nSurface high pressure, centered over southeastern Canada, has been \nin place for the past few days. An upper-level low passed overhead \nyesterday, allowing for the surface wind field to gain cyclonic \ncurvature. This has lead to weak lift within the boundary layer, \ncausing stratus trapped beneath an inversion to produce widespread \ndrizzle. Drizzle continues as of this writing (2am), and is expected \nto persist through the morning hours today.\n\nThe upper-level low has moved southeastward towards the Mid-\nAtlantic, and surface high pressure is beginning to nudge southward \nin its place. Broad subsidence and dry air is expected to return, \ncausing the low stratus to finally begin to dissipate. This process \nis likely to be slow, with mainly cloudy skies expected for most of \nthe day today. Due to this, we went below guidance for high \ntemperatures today. Clearing ramps up after sunset, especially east \nof a Lafayette to Terre Haute line.\n\nWinds become light and variable at times tonight, with perhaps a \ndominant northeasterly component. Some patchy fog is possible given \nthe moist boundary layer, but renewed dry air advection may mitigate \nthis potential. With clearing skies and light winds, the main effect \nwill be much cooler lows than previous nights. Temperatures in the \n40s are possible where clearing is most widespread. In urban areas, \nlows near 50 are more probable. Along the Illinois state line, cloud \ncover could persist long enough to keep lows in the 50s.\n\nFRIDAY THROUGH SUNDAY\n\nBy Friday, low cloud cover will be gone only to be replaced by high \ncirrus arriving from the northwest. A frontal system over the plains \nis expected to generate convection from Texas to North Dakota. \nThough precipitation remains far to our west, debris cirrus could \nbecome quite thick at times Friday as it spreads eastward...thick \nenough to keep high temperatures suppressed a few degrees. High \ntemperatures returning to the 70s is still the most likely outcome, \nthough we did nudge the forecast down a degree or two from guidance.\n\nHigh pressure strengthens its grip over the region this weekend, \nproviding enough of a buffer to keep thick cirrus at bay. Some thin \ncirrus may still try to creep into our western counties, however. \nWinds retain an east to northeast direction through the weekend, so \na rapid warm up is not expected. Nevertheless, increasing sunshine \nshould help temperatures gradually warm both Saturday and Sunday. \nHighs in the 70s are likely both days.\n\nNEXT WEEK\n\nGuidance is beginning to hint at a potent system developing over the \nPlains around the Tuesday timeframe. Current ensemble suite \ngenerally brings the low center to our northwest, with the trailing \ncold front passing through Indiana. Uncertainty remains regarding \nthe strength and track of the low, but enough of a signal is present \nto justify higher PoPs Tuesday night through early Thursday. \nThunderstorms are also possible, but deep warm moist advection \nappears limited despite the system's potential strength. For now, \nmost members of guidance show only low amounts of MLCAPE (under 500 \nJ/Kg). This system resembles a cold-season synoptic low with minimal \ninstability and high shear. So, despite the low MLCAPE, this system \nstill bears watching from a severe weather standpoint.",
